3/6/03

For simulations of pure liquids in BOSS 4.5, 
(1) The maximum number of atoms in a solvent molecule
    has been increased to 50.
(2) The Lennard-Jones and Coulombic contributions, ESLJ and ESCO,
    to the total solvent-solvent are now output, e.g, for OPLS-AA methanol:

 Solvent-Solvent Energy Components
 Run      L-J         Coulomb
  4     -433.967     -1823.797
  1     -433.505     -1863.756
  2     -429.166     -1822.612
  3     -410.251     -1839.221

                                            Standard Deviations
           5.596         9.581

                                                 Averages
        -426.722     -1837.347

    Note that:

    Etotal = ESLJ + ESCO + Eintramolecular

    ESLJ and ESCO are fully evaluated ca. 10 times during each
    Monte Carlo run and averaged. The Etotal and Eintramolecular
    are averaged for every configuration. Thus, there may be a little
    noise in the above equality, e.g., for methanol
    Etotal = -348.7
    ESLJ + ESCO + Eintra = -426.7 -1837.3 + 1916.8 = -347.2
 
    These quantities are all for the full system with N = 267 methanol
    molecules in the example, and note that 
 
    deltaHvap = Egas - Etotal/N + RT

    where Egas is the gas-phase energy for a single molecule, which 
    may be obtained running the xMCGAS script or see the MCgas test
    job for more details.

(3) The Z-matrix format for custom solvents has been changed to be the
    same as for solutes. Please see section 10.13 of the User's Manual.
    Any solute Z-matrix is readily converted to a solvent Z-matrix by
    executing the xSPM script. E.g., for the Z-matrix for ethanol, etoh.z,
    xSPM etoh
    Then the output file sum contains the desired solvent Z-matrix; just
    change the title line to only contain 5 characters.

(4) The printed output has been simplified for pure liquids by not
    outputing results for the dummy solute.
